  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows a trough of low pressure \nstretched from Central IL across central Indiana and then east to \nDayton. GOES19 shows an extensive area clouds remnants from earlier \nthunderstorms. Cloud tops continued to warm as showers for the most \npart continued to dissipate. Radar still shows scattered showers \nstretching from IA across IL to Central Indiana. Clouds had limited \ndaytime heating somewhat as most temperatures were in the 70s. Water \nVapor imagery shows ridging in place over the Mississippi Valley. \nThe ridge was allowing a tropical flow from the Pacific to stream up \nand around it before reaching Indiana and the Ohio valley. An \nembedded wave was found across IL and Indiana, pushing east within \nthe flow.\n\nTonight and Saturday...\n\nCloud cover across the area this afternoon is expected to remain \ninto the evening. This will prevent any additional diurnal \nconvection as convective temperatures will not be reached. None the \nless, clouds will continue to linger through the evening and \novernight, along with the lower level boundary remaining in place. \nForecast soundings show plentiful moisture through the column \novernight, with high pwats still available. However a forcing \nmechanism does not appear available much of the night. The HRRR \nsuggests another MCS pushing toward Indiana near 09Z and beginning \nto cross the state at and around 12Z. Diurnal trends would suggest a \ngradual dissipation through the morning hours. However, again \nforecast soundings still show abundant moisture with high pwats \nthrough the day. \n\nThus we will use low pops this evening, but will ramp pops higher \nacross the northern parts of the forecast area from late tonight \nthrough Saturday Morning. Due to saturated soils, this will require \nthe extension of the flood watch across the northern parts of the \nforecast area. Given the expected cloud cover, will trim highs on \nSaturday to lower 80s, but higher values will be possible across the \nsouthwest area, farther away from precipitation.\n\nSunday and Sunday Night...\n\nNorthwest flow looks to remains in place on Sunday and yet another \nwave looks to pass across Indiana within the flow. A cold front is \nexpected cross the state on Sunday and Central Indiana will spend \nSunday afternoon within the warm sector ahead of the front. Again \nforecast soundings show favorable CAPE in excess of 2400 J/KG and \nthe very high pwats near 2.2. Thus again, high pops will be needed \nin the afternoon and flood potential will still be in play.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nDry weather will be expected on Monday and Tuesday as high pressure \nremains across the area. Northwest flow will be in place aloft, but \nlittle in the way of forcing dynamics is expected to pass. \nSubsidence will be in play as weak ridging will be occurring \nupstream over the Rockies. The Canadian high pressure will allow \ntemperatures to return to near normal or slightly below normal \ntemperatures with less humidity. \n\nWednesday...\n\nBest chances for precipitation next week looks to be on Wednesday \ninto Wednesday Night. A moderate upper short wave within the flow is \nexpected to pass across the Great Lakes. This along with an \nassociated cold front is expected to pass across Indiana. With warm \nand more humid southerly winds in place across Indiana due to the \ndeparting high pressure system, ample moisture will be available. \nThus pops will be needed. At the moment, best timing looks to be in \nthe afternoon and evening, but given this many days away this timing \ncould change.\n\nThursday and Friday...\n\nDry weather looks to return for the end of the work week. The \nprevious cold front will push to the Ohio river by Thursday morning, \nallowing high pressure to build across Central Indiana for the end \nof the work week. Aloft, stronger ridging is expected over the \nRockies, that should result in more leeside subsidence over the \nupper midwest. A caveat is that a weak upper trough will still be \npassing across the Great Lakes that could provide some forcing.",
